  "intro": {
    "text": "`input(\"Your name? \")` shows the text in the brackets (the **prompt**), waits for the user to type a line and press Enter, and gives back what they typed.\n\nWhat comes back is **always text**, even when the user types digits. To do math with it, convert it: `age = int(input(\"Age? \"))`. Python runs the inside first: input gives text, then int turns it into a number.\n\nThe prompt does not add a space after itself, so end it with one.\n\nIn these lessons the user's answers are on the **keyboard** panel: each `input()` takes the next one and crosses it off. When you run your own code, you type the answers in a box, one per line.",
    "analogy": "input() is like a waiter taking an order. The waiter asks a question (the prompt), writes down exactly what you say on a note, and brings back the note. The note is always words on paper, even if you said a number, so the kitchen has to read it as a number itself."
  },
